    "card_no": '1',
    "term":'<p class="qz-txt"><b>A 17-year-old male presents to the emergency department (ED) for progressive chest pain and shortness of breath over 3 weeks. He notes the shortness of breath has been worsening, especially at night, and he has a persistent dry cough. He has had occasional fevers and notes unintended weight loss of 5 kg. On physical exam, there are decreased breath sounds over the left upper chest with no wheezing as well as supraclavicular lymphadenopathy.</b></p><div class="key1"><p><b>What is the next step in his management?</b></p></div>',
    "termaudio":"",
    "defnaudio":"",
    "imgurl":"",
    "imgalt":"",
    "imageonly":true,
    "defination" :[
      {
        "title":"",
        "phase": '<p class="dmn"><b>Domain 9: Solid Tumors, General Considerations, Clinical Presentations</b></p><p class="ans1"><b>Obtain a chest x-ray to evaluate for possible mediastinal mass.</b> In this patient with rapid onset of shortness of breath, weight loss, and night sweats, malignancy should be high on the differential. In rapidly occurring symptoms (weeks to months), differentials include non-seminomatous germ cell tumor (NSGCT) or lymphoblastic non-Hodgkin lymphoma (NHL). Further&#x00A0;workup may include lactate dehydrogenase (LDH), &#x03B1;-fetoprotein (AFP), and &#x03B2;-human chorionic gonadotropin (&#x03B2;-hCG) with eventual biopsy.</p><div class="key"><p><b>Key Point:</b> Obtain a chest x-ray in patients with rapid onset of symptoms concerning for mediastinal mass such as onset of respiratory symptoms over weeks to months.</p></div><div class="suggested_readings"><p><u>Suggested Readings</u></p><p>Carter BW, Marom EM, Detterbeck FC. Approaching the patient with an anterior mediastinal mass: a guide for clinicians. <i>J&#x00A0;Thorac Oncol</i>. 2014;9(9 supplement 2):S102-S109. doi:<a href="https://doi.org/10.1097/JTO.0000000000000294">10.1097/JTO.0000000000000294</a></p><p>Locke R, Comfort R, Kubba H. When does an enlarged cervical lymph node in a child need excision? A systematic review. <i>Int&#x00A0;J&#x00A0;Pediatr</i> <i>Otorhinolaryngol</i>. 2014;78(3):393-401.</p></div>'
      }
    ]
