    "imageonly":true,
    "defination" :[
      {
        "title":"",
        "phase": '<p class="dmn"><b>Domain 9: Solid Tumors, General Considerations, Clinical Presentations</b></p><p class="ans1"><b>Obstruction of blood flow of the superior vena cava (SVC) resulting in decreased venous return from the head and neck to the heart (SVC syndrome).</b> Mediastinal masses may present with compression of vascular or airway structures and can be life threatening. SVC syndrome presents as edema and plethora of the face and neck due to venous occlusion. Wheezing and dyspnea can occur if there is airway compromise, dysphagia can occur with esophageal compression. Cardiac compromise from either obstruction of flow or pericardial effusion causing tamponade can also occur. Patients with SVC syndrome should be evaluated emergently, with avoidance of anesthesia and sedation if possible.</p><div class="key"><p><b>Key Point:</b> SVC syndrome presents in patients with mediastinal mass and can cause airway, venous, or esophageal compression.</p></div><div class="suggested_readings"><p><u>Suggested Readings</u></p><p>Nossair FA-O, Schoettler P, Starr J, et al. Pediatric superior vena cava syndrome: an evidence-based systematic review of the&#x00A0;literature. <i>Pediatr Blood Cancer</i>. 2018;65(9):1-8.</p><p>Twist CJ, Link MP. Assessment of lymphadenopathy in children. <i>Pediatr Clin North Am</i>. 2002;49(5):1009-1025. doi:<a href="https://doi.org/10.1016/S0031-3955(02)00038-X">10.1016/S0031-3955(02)00038-X</a></p><p>Wright K, Digby GC, Gyawali B, et al. Malignant superior Vena Cava syndrome: a scoping review. <i>J Thorac Oncol</i>. 2023;18(10):1268-1276. doi:<a href="https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jtho.2023.04.019">10&#8203;.1016/j.jtho.2023.04.019</a></p></div>'
      }
    ]
