    "imgalt":"",
    "imageonly":true,
    "defination" :[
      {
        "title":"",
        "phase": '<p class="dmn"><b>Domain 9: Solid Tumors, General Considerations, Diagnostic Imaging</b></p><p class="ans1"><b>A well-circumscribed cystic mass in the cerebellum with an avidly enhancing mural lesion.</b> Pilocytic astrocytoma is the most common primary brain tumor in children and accounts for a third of gliomas in children. It most commonly occurs in the cerebellar hemisphere and lacks significant vasogenic edema. Other pediatric supratentorial brain tumor includes pleomorphic xanthoastrocytoma (solid components have avid enhancement), diffuse astrocytoma (ill-defined without contrast enhancement), and ependymoma (EPN; calcifications, hemorrhage, and often parenchymal).</p><div class="key"><p><b>Key Point:</b> Pilocytic astrocytoma is the most common primary supratentorial tumor in children presenting as a cystic mass with enhancing mural nodule.</p></div><div class="suggested_readings"><p><u>Suggested Readings</u></p><p>Nikam RM, Yue X, Kaur G, et al. Advanced neuroimaging approaches to pediatric brain tumors. <i>Cancers</i>. 2022;14(14):1-34. doi:<a href="https://doi.org/10.3390/cancers14143401">10.3390/cancers&#8203;14143401</a></p><p>Zamora C, Huisman TA, Izbudak I. Supratentorial tumors in pediatric patients. <i>Neuroimaging Clin N Am</i>. 2017;27(1):39-67.</p></div>'
      }
    ]
